# Static analysis for the native split's C++ (app/native-split/src and test).
#
# Ported from avi/any-stt's .clang-tidy, which mirrors avi/youtube-time-manager:
# .clang-format owns layout (their @stylistic/eslint half) and this file owns
# logic and correctness (their oxlint half). oxlint runs
# `categories.correctness: error`; the C++ equivalent is bugprone-* +
# clang-analyzer-* + cert-* + concurrency-*, as errors.
#
# Run:  pwsh tools/lint.ps1  (check)   /   pwsh tools/lint.ps1 -Fix   (apply fixes)
#
# ---- their .oxlintrc.json rule by rule, and what answers it here -----------
#   categories.correctness  -> bugprone-*, cert-*, clang-analyzer-*, concurrency-*
#   prefer-const            -> misc-const-correctness (values only, as below)
#   id-length min 3 + list  -> readability-identifier-length + IgnoredNames
#   no-nested-ternary       -> readability-avoid-nested-conditional-operator
#   curly: all              -> .clang-format InsertBraces
#   no-unused-vars          -> misc-unused-*, clang-analyzer-deadcode.DeadStores
#   no-restricted-globals   -> bugprone-unsafe-functions.CustomFunctions
#   import/first, dupes     -> readability-duplicate-include
#   @stylistic/*, max-len   -> .clang-format (Google, ColumnLimit 80)
#   spaced-comment: always  -> .clang-format SpacesInLineCommentPrefix
#
# Deliberately OFF, and why (the same list as any-stt, for the same reasons):
#   performance-no-int-to-ptr, bugprone-multi-level-implicit-pointer-conversion
#     — this DLL binds msedge.dll by RVA: every Edge function is an
#       integer-to-pointer cast and every field read goes through raw offsets.
#       The rule is right in general and wrong for every binding here.
#   readability-implicit-bool-conversion, readability-braces-around-statements,
#   readability-magic-numbers
#     — braces are clang-format's InsertBraces; the other two would drown the
#       real findings (offsets.h is nothing but reversed magic numbers).
#   misc-include-cleaner — Win32 headers have hard ordering dependencies, the
#       same reason SortIncludes is off in .clang-format.
#   readability-identifier-naming — offsets.h names each RVA and offset after
#       the reversed symbol (`Class_Method`, `CLASS_field`) so it greps against
#       the PDB and docs/re, and the hook pairs are `h_` (detour) / `o_`
#       (original) on purpose. CLAUDE.md's table stays a review concern.
#   cert-dcl50-cpp — Log() is a printf-style variadic on purpose.
#   bugprone-unchecked-string-to-number-conversion, misc-no-recursion
#       (the layout tree is recursive by nature), the rest as in any-stt.
#
# ---- C++ best practices, held once applied ----------------------------------
# Each practice below was applied to the whole tree, and its check is an error
# from then on, so a regression fails the gate instead of waiting for review:
#   no C casts                  -> cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-cstyle-cast,
#                                  google-readability-casting
#   nullptr, never NULL or 0    -> modernize-use-nullptr
#   override on every override  -> modernize-use-override
#   default member initializers -> modernize-use-default-member-init,
#                                  cppcoreguidelines-prefer-member-initializer
#   explicit converting ctors   -> google-explicit-constructor
#   `using`, `= default/delete` -> modernize-use-using, modernize-use-equals-*
#   C++ headers, bool literals  -> modernize-deprecated-headers,
#                                  modernize-use-bool-literals,
#                                  modernize-redundant-void-arg
#   no dynamic throw() specs    -> modernize-use-noexcept
#   virtual dtors, no slicing   -> cppcoreguidelines-virtual-class-destructor,
#                                  cppcoreguidelines-slicing
#   const member functions      -> readability-make-member-function-const,
#                                  readability-non-const-parameter
#   const is never cast away    -> cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-const-cast
#     (edge::Field has a const overload for fields we only read)
#   `const auto*` for const     -> readability-qualified-auto
#   [[nodiscard]] results       -> modernize-use-nodiscard (const member
#                                  functions); by hand, every function of the
#                                  pure headers and every call that reports
#                                  success (verify, InstallAll, LoadFromFile)
#   no raw new/delete or malloc -> cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory,
#                                  cppcoreguidelines-no-malloc,
#                                  modernize-make-unique
#     (Win32 handles have owners in handles.h for the same reason; no check
#     can see a CloseHandle a path forgot, so that one stays a review concern.)
#   nothing left uninitialised  -> cppcoreguidelines-init-variables,
#                                  cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-member-init
#     (and every struct member has a default initializer, the Edge ABI
#     mirrors too: an initializer is not layout)
#   the rule of five            -> cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions
#   noexcept moves, dtors, swap -> performance-noexcept-* (on, from
#                                  performance-*); noexcept on the pure,
#                                  non-allocating helpers is by hand
#   starts_with / ends_with     -> modernize-use-starts-ends-with
#   one declaration a statement -> readability-isolate-declaration
#   empty(), not size() > 0     -> readability-container-size-empty
#   no silent narrowing         -> bugprone-narrowing-conversions (on, from
#                                  bugprone-*: an int that becomes a float, or
#                                  an unsigned that becomes signed, is cast)
Checks: >
  -*,
  cppcoreguidelines-init-variables,
  cppcoreguidelines-no-malloc,
  cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory,
  cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-member-init,
  cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions,
  modernize-make-unique,
  cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-const-cast,
  cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-cstyle-cast,
  cppcoreguidelines-prefer-member-initializer,
  cppcoreguidelines-slicing,
  cppcoreguidelines-virtual-class-destructor,
  google-explicit-constructor,
  google-readability-casting,
  modernize-deprecated-headers,
  modernize-redundant-void-arg,
  modernize-use-bool-literals,
  modernize-use-default-member-init,
  modernize-use-equals-default,
  modernize-use-equals-delete,
  modernize-use-nodiscard,
  modernize-use-noexcept,
  modernize-use-nullptr,
  modernize-use-override,
  modernize-use-starts-ends-with,
  modernize-use-using,
  readability-make-member-function-const,
  readability-non-const-parameter,
  readability-container-size-empty,
  readability-isolate-declaration,
  readability-qualified-auto,
  bugprone-*,
  cert-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  concurrency-*,
  misc-*,
  performance-*,
  readability-avoid-nested-conditional-operator,
  readability-identifier-length,
  readability-const-return-type,
  readability-delete-null-pointer,
  readability-duplicate-include,
  readability-inconsistent-declaration-parameter-name,
  readability-misleading-indentation,
  readability-misplaced-array-index,
  readability-redundant-control-flow,
  readability-redundant-declaration,
  readability-redundant-preprocessor,
  readability-redundant-string-cstr,
  readability-simplify-boolean-expr,
  readability-string-compare,
  readability-function-cognitive-complexity,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-multi-level-implicit-pointer-conversion,
  -bugprone-reserved-identifier,
  -bugprone-unchecked-string-to-number-conversion,
  -cert-dcl37-c,
  -cert-dcl50-cpp,
  -cert-dcl51-cpp,
  -cert-err33-c,
  -cert-err34-c,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -misc-no-recursion,
  -misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,
  -misc-use-anonymous-namespace,
  -misc-use-internal-linkage,
  -performance-enum-size,
  -performance-no-int-to-ptr

# Only our own headers; never report inside the Windows SDK or external/
# (MinHook, vendored).
HeaderFilterRegex: 'native-split[/\\](src|test)[/\\].*\.h$'
# Everything is an error EXCEPT cognitive-complexity: that mirrors fallow's
# maxCognitive:30, and it is report-only — it names the next decomposition
# targets without failing the gate.
WarningsAsErrors: '*,-readability-function-cognitive-complexity'
FormatStyle: file
CheckOptions:
  readability-function-cognitive-complexity.Threshold: '30'  # == fallow maxCognitive

  # oxlint's `no-restricted-globals` — name a thing, say why, name what to use
  # instead. Here it is the writes with no destination size: every one of them
  # is a buffer overrun waiting for a protocol field to grow, and the tree
  # already writes the bounded form everywhere else. `<n>,<replacement>,<why>`.
  bugprone-unsafe-functions.CustomFunctions: >-
    ^::sprintf$,snprintf,it cannot see the size of what it writes into;
    ^::strcpy$,strcpy_s,it cannot see the size of what it writes into;
    ^::strcat$,strcat_s,it cannot see the size of what it writes into;
    ^::wcscpy$,wcscpy_s,it cannot see the size of what it writes into;
    ^::wcscat$,wcscat_s,it cannot see the size of what it writes into;
    ^::gets$,fgets,it cannot see the size of what it writes into

  # oxlint's `id-length: [error, {min: 3, exceptions: [...]}]`, in C++. The
  # minimum is theirs; the exception list is CLAUDE.md's — "established platform
  # idioms that would read worse spelled out":
  #   hr hwnd pid tid rc   Win32/COM, and unreadable expanded
  #   i j                  a bare loop counter (a MEANINGFUL index is named)
  #   ok to                a success flag, and the `from`/`to` pair
  #   x y                  a coordinate pair (gfx::Point's own names)
  # Anything else at one or two characters is a name that was not chosen.
  readability-identifier-length.MinimumVariableNameLength: '3'
  readability-identifier-length.MinimumParameterNameLength: '3'
  readability-identifier-length.IgnoredVariableNames: '^(hr|hwnd|pid|tid|rc|ok|to|i|j|x|y)$'
  readability-identifier-length.IgnoredParameterNames: '^(hr|hwnd|pid|tid|rc|ok|to|i|j|x|y)$'

  # oxlint's `prefer-const`. Values only: a `const` reference or pointer is a
  # different type, not a stricter binding, so the reference/pointer arms would
  # be changing signatures rather than locking locals down.
  #
  # REPORT ONLY — the Transform options are off on purpose. Its fixer writes
  # EAST const (`DWORD const x`) and this tree is west const throughout, so an
  # auto-fix would have the linter quietly introduce the second style it exists
  # to prevent. The finding names the variable; write `const` in front of the
  # type yourself. clang-format cannot put it back either: a Win32 typedef is
  # not a token it knows to be a type (see QualifierAlignment there).
  misc-const-correctness.AnalyzeValues: 'true'
  misc-const-correctness.AnalyzeReferences: 'false'
  misc-const-correctness.WarnPointersAsValues: 'false'
  misc-const-correctness.TransformValues: 'false'
  misc-const-correctness.TransformReferences: 'false'
  misc-const-correctness.TransformPointersAsValues: 'false'
